Stoer-Wagner: maximum-adjacency ordering with a binary heap keyed (connectivity desc, dense id asc) and lazy deletion - O(E + V log V) per phase, O(V E + V^2 log V) total (module quotients of a few thousand units are well inside it). Merged vertices tracked by a union-find with members lists; each phase records the phase cut (the last-added super-vertex's members vs the rest) and its weight; the global min is the minimum (ties: the earliest phase). A disconnected graph yields weight 0 with one component as the side (the first phase whose last vertex is unreachable). Self weights ignored (they never cross a cut). Dinic: undirected edges become two arcs of capacity w each; multiple sources / sinks attach to a super source / sink with capacity u64::MAX; BFS level graph + blocking flow through an explicit DFS stack with per-node current-arc pointers (no recursion); saturating adds. Min cut side = nodes reachable from the source in the residual graph; cut_pairs = original pairs with exactly one end on that side. Neighbour iteration uses WeightedGraph's sorted lists, so both algorithms are deterministic.

