impl TimelineBuilder { pub fn new(first: &DeltaSide<'_>) -> Self; pub fn push(&mut self, prev: &DeltaSide<'_>, next: &DeltaSide<'_>, moves: &[(NodeId, NodeId)]); pub fn finish(self) -> Timeline; }
```
new(first): DeltaSide's fields are private to algo::delta, so the builder never reads them: it runs graph_delta(&DeltaSide::default(), first, &DeltaOptions::default()) and opens every added node / edge at rev 0 (an empty before-side makes everything 'added'). push(prev, next, moves) = rev += 1; d = graph_delta(prev, next, DeltaOptions { content_cells: &[], moves }); close (until_rev = rev) every open edge in d.removed_edges (keyed as in prev, which is how open keys are held) and every open node in d.removed_nodes; then remap every remaining open key through d.moved_nodes (before -> after; a node's span records (rev, old id) in prior_ids); then open every d.added_edges / d.added_nodes at rev. Only a builder holding two snapshots at a time is needed, so memory is O(V + E) of the widest pair, not O(N x E). finish(): open items get until_rev None; output sorted by (from_rev, key) for edges and (from_rev, id) for nodes - BTreeMaps throughout, keyed on raw integers (EdgeKey's Ord), so the result is a pure function of the inputs. A snapshot the caller could not build is simply not pushed (the caller records the skip).

