packets.implementation:
DESIGN: the owner of every call made while evaluating a package var's initialiser is the var's STATE_VAR. It exists for every spec that holds a call (state_var_is_noise skips only a lone literal primitive), callers already bind CALLS to it, and A7.6 already uses it as a ref `from`. The MODULE would pool every var's callees on one node and cut the caller -> var -> callee path; a FUNCTION would invent a declaration Go does not have; Go's own `init` order is not modelled. One walker serves both shapes: the initialiser node is visited exactly as a statement child of a function body is, so a direct call and a call inside a literal it holds go through the same CallSite / detector code as in a function.
1. collect_calls_at<'t>(node: TsNode<'t>, src, from, receiver_var, repo, file_rel, acc, closures: &mut Vec<TsNode<'t>>): the body of collect_calls_in's loop, unchanged, applied to `node`: record_body_locals(node, ..); if call_expression -> classify_call -> CallSite { from, qualifier, line: line_at(node) } plus try_detect_go_endpoint / try_detect_gorm_access / try_detect_go_provider_set; if func_literal -> closures.push(node) (not entered) else collect_calls_in(node, ..). collect_calls_in(node, ..) = `for child in node.named_children(..) { collect_calls_at(child, ..) }`. No function body changes: the same children are visited in the same order.
2. emit_state_var_spec, inside the per-name loop after the DEFINES push, replacing `collect_provider_sets_in(*value, src, id, acc)`: `if spec.kind() == "var_spec" && let Some(value) = values.get(i) { let before = acc.calls.len(); let mut lits = Vec::new(); collect_calls_at(*value, src, id, None, repo, file_rel, acc, &mut lits); let direct = acc.calls.len() - before; let (b, c) = collect_closure_calls(lits, src, id, None, repo, file_rel, acc); acc.var_init_calls += direct; acc.var_literal_bodies += b; acc.var_literal_calls += c; if direct + b > 0 { acc.var_call_owners += 1 } }`. The walk starts at the VALUE, never the var_spec: record_body_locals' `var_spec` arm would otherwise record the package var as a local of its own STATE_VAR (CA.2a records it on the MODULE scope through record_package_vars, unchanged). For a const_spec the old provider-set walk is not needed either (a constant initialiser cannot call wire / fx).
3. Pairing (unchanged from HEAD's provider-set walk): names and values pair by position, so `var a, b = f(), g()` gives a -> f and b -> g; one multi-value initialiser `var lo, hi = bounds()` pairs with the first name only (lo -> bounds; hi owns nothing); each spec of a grouped `var ( .. )` block is its own emit_state_var_spec call.
4. collect_closure_calls keeps its body and returns `(bodies, calls)`; visit_function / visit_method do `let (b, c) = collect_closure_calls(..); acc.closure_bodies += b; acc.closure_calls += c;`, so CA.1's `[go-calls] func-literal` line keeps its numbers for every file. acc.func_literal_handlers is empty for a package var (no route walk runs there), so every queued literal is drained; nested literals are drained FIFO exactly as for a function.
5. What a package var yields, all `from` the STATE_VAR: CallSites at each call's own 0-based row (LC.3b site line, so the CALLS edge's EVIDENCE line is the call row): the initialiser's direct calls first in tree order, then each literal body's; the literals' parameters / body locals as locals of the STATE_VAR (record_params / record_body_locals, so CA.2b types `repo.Save()` inside `var onSave = func(repo *store.Repo) {..}`; RecvScope.func = the STATE_VAR is accepted, it only excludes a MODULE `from`); the three detectors (client ENDPOINT, GORM access, wire / fx / dig INJECTS) attach to the STATE_VAR, the INJECTS refs exactly once and in the same order as collect_provider_sets_in pushed them (pre-order, a call before its arguments). External / stdlib / builtin calls (`errors.New`, `make`, `time.Duration(5)`) stay unresolved CallSites, as in a function body. resolve_calls finds the caller's module through enclosing_home_module (the STATE_VAR's nav parent is its file MODULE), so no graph change is needed.
6. Marker, per file, only when var_init_calls + var_literal_bodies > 0, printed after CA.1's `[go-calls] func-literal` line: `[go-calls] package-var initialisers calls=<d> literal bodies=<b> literal calls=<c> vars=<v> in <file>`.
7. TESTS (parsers/code/go/src/lib.rs mod tests, parse_file with module prefix `example.com/hooks`): (a) package_var_literal_calls_belong_to_the_var - the fixture's hooks/hooks.go: CallSites from STATE_VAR `hooks::hooks::newStore` == [Attribute{store, Open}], `timeNow` == [Bare(clock)], `onEvict` == [Attribute{store, Evict}, Bare(audit)] with each call's 0-based row, `handlers` == [Bare(flush)]; no CallSite from the MODULE id; no FUNCTION node whose qname ends in `newStore` or `onEvict`. (b) nested_package_var_literals_are_drained - `var getZone = func() string { once.Do(func() { fetch() }); return z }` -> CallSites from getZone are [Attribute{once, Do}, Bare(fetch)] in that order (body first, nested literal after). (c) package_var_literal_params_are_locals_of_the_var - `var onSave = func(repo *store.Repo) { repo.Save() }` -> nav.local_types[onSave id] == {repo: "store.Repo"}. (d) package_var_initialiser_calls_belong_to_the_var - the fixture's hooks.go: `defaultStore` == [Attribute{store, Open}] at row 20, `limit` == [Bare(clamp)], `ttl` == [Bare(clamp)], `lo` == [Bare(bounds)], `hi` has none; and `var svc = New()
var n = 3
var ProviderSet = wire.NewSet(NewA)
const c = len("ab")` (with `import "github.com/google/wire"`) -> svc == [Bare(New)], ProviderSet == [Attribute{wire, NewSet}], no CallSite from any other id (n is noise-gated, the const is not walked), and the INJECTS refs are exactly [(ProviderSet, Bare(NewA))] (once, not doubled). (e) initialiser_walk_records_no_locals - `var svc = New()` and `var onSave = func(repo *store.Repo) {}` -> nav.local_types has the MODULE scope (svc: "New()", from CA.2a) and the onSave scope only; no scope keyed by svc. The existing wire_newset_var_emits_injects_from_state_var (INJECTS [(set, NewA), (set, NewB)], an exact vec: it fails if the walk doubles them) and package_vars_use_the_module_scope (local_types.len() == 1) stay green unchanged.
